SEG = 'PSY'
CAT = 'hhaPsych'
Q = [
('A patient with dementia asks the same question again and again. What does the aide do?',
 'Answer calmly each time', ['Gently remind her she asked', 'Explain her memory loss kindly', 'Answer only the first time'],
 'People with dementia often cannot remember asking. A calm answer reduces anxiety, while pointing out the repetition or her memory loss, however gently, can upset her.'),
('A patient with dementia becomes upset at bath time. What does the aide do?',
 'Stop and try again later', ['Finish quickly and gently', 'Explain each step firmly', 'Ask her son to help hold her'],
 'Pushing care on an upset person with dementia increases distress and risk of injury. Pausing and returning later often works.'),
('How does an aide speak to a patient who is hard of hearing?',
 'Face her and speak clearly', ['Speak louder into her ear', 'Use a slow, high voice', 'Exaggerate each word'],
 "Facing her lets her see the aide's lips and expression. A lower, clear voice is easier to hear than a high or shouted one, and exaggerated words are harder to lip-read."),
("While cleaning a blind patient's living room, the aide moves a chair. What does she do after?",
 'Put it back where it was', ['Tell her where it is now', 'Leave it where it is safer', 'Push it against the wall'],
 'A person who is blind finds her way by where things always are. Returning furniture to its usual place prevents trips and falls.'),
('A patient fasts during daylight for a religious holiday, and her care plan allows it. What does the aide do?',
 'Offer meals after sunset', ['Offer a light lunch anyway', 'Ask the RN to end the fast', 'Serve meals at usual times'],
 'The DC outline calls for care sensitive to spiritual beliefs. When the plan allows the fast, the aide adjusts meal times and reports any signs of illness.'),
('A patient wants to pray before her morning care. What does the aide do?',
 'Give her time to pray', ['Begin care quietly meanwhile', 'Ask her to pray after care', 'Join in her prayer aloud'],
 'Respecting spiritual practices is part of psychosocial care. Care can begin once she has finished.'),
('Which is one of the commonly described stages of grief?',
 'Bargaining', ['Resolution', 'Recovery', 'Rebellion'],
 'The stages usually described are denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance. Not everyone goes through them in order.'),
('A dying patient wants to talk about her death. What does the aide do?',
 'Listen and let her talk', ['Reassure her she may improve', 'Remind her to rest now', 'Change to a lighter topic'],
 'Listening supports her emotional needs. False reassurance, telling her to rest or changing the subject shuts her out.'),
('A patient says she has no reason to live anymore. What does the aide do?',
 'Tell her supervisor right away', ['Note it for the next RN visit', 'Remind her what she enjoys', 'Keep it private, as she asked'],
 'Statements like this can signal suicide risk and are reported at once. Keeping it secret or waiting for the next visit can cost her life.'),
('Before helping a patient use the toilet, the aide:',
 'Closes the door or curtain', ['Stays beside her throughout', 'Leaves the door ajar', 'Turns off the light'],
 "42 CFR 484.80 lists respect for the patient's privacy, and the skills listing provides privacy with a door, curtain or screen."),
('The aide is caring for a 4-year-old. How does she explain care?',
 'Simply, before she does it', ['To the parent only', 'After the care is done', 'In correct medical terms'],
 'Young children understand short, simple explanations and fear what is unexplained.'),
('Which change is usually part of normal aging?',
 'Slower reaction time', ['Sudden confusion', 'Loss of most memory', 'New incontinence overnight'],
 'Reactions slow with age. Sudden confusion, severe memory loss or new incontinence are changes to report, not normal aging.'),
('An older patient is suddenly confused today. This is:',
 'A change to report', ['Normal aging', 'Tiredness only', 'Expected after a nap'],
 'Sudden confusion can mean infection, dehydration or a stroke. 42 CFR 484.80 requires aides to report changes in condition.'),
("A patient's son criticizes how the aide made the bed. What does the aide do?",
 'Stay calm and listen', ['Defend how she did it', 'Ask him to do it himself', 'Keep working; ignore him'],
 'Calm listening defuses most complaints. If a concern needs action, the aide passes it on to the supervisor.'),
('Which body language tells a patient the aide is listening?',
 'Eye contact and nodding', ['Arms crossed, leaning back', 'Writing notes while she talks', 'Standing by the door'],
 'Facing the patient, eye contact where culturally comfortable, and nodding show attention. Closed posture or distractions say the aide is not interested.'),
('A patient asks to be called by a different name than the one on her chart. What does the aide do?',
 'Use the name she asks for', ['Use the chart name to be safe', 'Use her last name only', 'Ask her family which to use'],
 'The DC outline calls for care sensitive to preferences and gender identity. Using the name she chooses shows respect.'),
('After a stroke, a patient struggles to find words. What does the aide do?',
 'Give her time to answer', ['Finish her sentences', 'Speak louder to her', 'Speak slowly and loudly'],
 'Patience lets her express herself. Finishing sentences can guess wrong and frustrate her; her hearing is not the problem.'),
('A patient who lives alone seems lonely. What can the aide do?',
 'Talk with her during care', ['Give her a personal number', 'Visit on her days off', 'Stay past the visit time'],
 'Conversation during care meets social needs within the job. Personal numbers, unpaid visits and staying past visit time cross professional boundaries.'),
('A female patient asks that only women bathe her. What does the aide, a man, do?',
 'Pass it on to the agency', ['Bathe her with her daughter present', 'Skip all bathing for now', 'Bathe her with the lights low'],
 'The preference is respected and the agency changes the assignment. Skipping care or working around her wish is not respectful or safe.'),
("A patient's family uses a home remedy the aide has never seen. What does the aide do?",
 'Respect it; tell the nurse', ['Ask them to stop using it', 'Look it up and decide', 'Give it when they ask'],
 'Cultural practices are respected. If something might interact with her care or cause harm, the aide tells the nurse rather than acting alone.'),
]
